{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS2KAnAqEBF1g5PkWyTF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the code for three causes, but I couldn't test any of it against the real Intervals.icu. The scope fix in particular rests on an inference. The causes are written up in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n1. **Connect button gave 401:** it was a plain link. A link navigation can't send the `Authorization: Bearer` header, because the token lives in localStorage and only the app's `fetch` wrapper attaches it. The button now fetches `/api/intervals/auth/url` with the header, gets the authorize URL back as JSON, and sends the browser there. The old `/api/intervals/auth/init` redirect still works for API clients.\n\n2. **Authorize step returned 400 `invalid_request`:** I reverted `SCOPES` in `config.py` to `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`, the scopes of the only grant that worked (Rivera, 2026-09-19). The calendar scopes came in with the 2026-10-02 calendar sync, and the 400 started after that. Intervals' error body doesn't name the bad parameter, so this is inferred from timing. If it still fails, check the calendar scope names and approval on the client registration. Calendar sync will need that sorted out separately.\n\n3. **\"Expired, reconnect\" after an hour:** Intervals' token response has neither `expires_in` nor `refresh_token`. The code assumed a 3600-second lifetime, and with no refresh token it then marked the connection expired. Tokens with no stated expiry are now treated as non-expiring, and the refresher skips them. A refresh response that omits `expires_in` no longer breaks the refresher either.\n\nI also checked that `server.py` compiles. I didn't deploy, publish or send anything outside this directory."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":88,"cache_read_input_tokens":31922,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_in